=== PROPOSAL ===
  ABSTAINED - no permitted action fits the evidence
  if wrong    : The abstention is wrong if change history actually does hold a prior image tag for ad that this briefing's change_history result failed to render — tr_35644441905b shows the prior reference as absent (None -> ghcr.io/open-telemetry/demo:2.2.0-ad-hotfix.2), which is the whole basis for declining. A second query against the change log, or any deployment manifest naming ad's pre-09:39 tag, would falsify it and make rollback_image the right call immediately. It would also be wrong if container-level evidence (restart count, exit code, image-pull status) showed ad's container repeatedly starting and dying on the *same* image rather than never coming up — that would be process state and would open restart_service.
  rests on    : tr_35644441905b, tr_8337203492c7, tr_79bc6944e185
  execution   : not executed - a proposal is a claim, never the change (ADR-0028 §1)

context: 34602 pushed / 8083 pulled (pull rate 0.1894), 2 section(s) dropped

=== VERDICT ===
  fault class : bad_deploy
  fix class   : rollback
  confidence  : high
  evidence    : tr_35644441905b, tr_8337203492c7, tr_79bc6944e185, tr_637a00c2a3a4, tr_2b011490442f
  root cause  : At 09:39:07 platform automation updated ad's container image reference to a hotfix-tagged build (tr_35644441905b). Ad's own log stream ends one second earlier with an orderly JVM-driven gRPC server shutdown and a clean shutdown confirmation, and then nothing at all for the remaining eight minutes of the window (tr_8337203492c7) — the old container was stopped for the rollout and no replacement ever came up logging. From 09:39:47 onward frontend's only error is a sustained gRPC code 14 with connect EHOSTUNREACH to a single peer endpoint on the ad port, with the resolved address present and no connection ever established (tr_79bc6944e185), and traces show the failing frontend client spans are childless leaves on the AdService/GetAds call that error in well under a millisecond while every other frontend dependency in the same traces succeeds (tr_637a00c2a3a4). The failure mechanism is therefore that the artifact deployed to ad is not serving: the rollout tore down the running instance and the new image never established a listening endpoint, so ad's address is unreachable and every /api/data request through frontend and frontend-proxy fails upward.
  OPEN        : No container-level evidence was gathered for ad — restart counts, exit codes, OOM kills, or image-pull failures — so whether the new image crash-looped, failed to pull, or started and died silently is unestablished.
  OPEN        : The prior image reference is recorded as absent (None -> hotfix), so this is a first-recorded pin rather than a diffable bump; the exact artifact to roll back to is not named by the evidence.
  OPEN        : Ad's log result returned only the oldest 8 and newest 32 lines, leaving roughly 09:14:56-09:37:58 unexamined; an earlier degradation or an intervening restart attempt in that gap cannot be excluded.
  OPEN        : 172.18.0.19:9555 was never confirmed to be ad's address by any result — the trace output exposed no span attributes, so the IP-to-service mapping is inferred from the failing logical dependency rather than observed.
  OPEN        : Ad latency, memory, CPU and restart metrics were never queried; only the error-ratio template was returned for every ad metric dispatch.
  OPEN        : Frontend's own error-ratio baseline window returned zero samples, so no pre-incident comparison at the frontend tier exists and the onset time at that tier rests on logs and traces alone.
  OPEN        : Six of the nine services in the blast radius were never dispatched on; whether anything else depends on ad and is silently degraded is unknown.

